      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we start with the given temperature function:\n\n\\[ T(t) = -t^2 + 12t + 50 \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( t \\) such that \\( T(t) = 77 \\). This gives us the equation:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77 \\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange this equation to standard quadratic form:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0 \\]\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNext, we multiply the entire equation by -1 to simplify:\n\n\\[ t^2 - 12t + 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula, which is given by:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\]\n\nHere, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\). Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2} \\]\n\nThis gives us two potential solutions for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9 \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\]\n\nThe solutions to the equation are \\( t = 9 \\) and \\( t = 3 \\). The largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]",
